# Reproduces exactly what VS Code's C/C++ extension already applies on save:
# its "Visual Studio" fallback style (LLVM with 4-space Allman braces and no
# column limit). Pinning it here means the formatting survives an extension
# default change and can be checked from the command line:
#
#     clang-format --dry-run --Werror csrc/*.c include/*.h
#
# Verified byte-for-byte against an editor-formatted file with clang-format
# 20.1.0: running it over csrc/unk_1811C.c is a no-op.
#
# Formatting cannot affect matching -- whitespace does not reach the compiler's
# output -- so the only thing at stake is keeping hand edits and format-on-save
# from fighting over whole files.
BasedOnStyle: LLVM
UseTab: Never
IndentWidth: 4
BreakBeforeBraces: Allman
AllowShortIfStatementsOnASingleLine: false
IndentCaseLabels: false
ColumnLimit: 0

# The one deliberate departure from the editor default, and the reason this
# file is worth committing: LLVM sorts includes, and sorting include/common.h
# puts libcd.h ahead of sys/types.h, which fails to compile ("parse error
# before `u_char'"). The order there is load-bearing. Verified by doing it.
SortIncludes: Never
